{"results":[{"url":"https://www.usnews.com/news/world/articles/2026-09-28/explainer-why-has-the-drive-to-verify-indias-billion-voters-put-poll-body-under-scrutiny","final_url":"https://www.usnews.com/news/world/articles/2026-09-28/explainer-why-has-the-drive-to-verify-indias-billion-voters-put-poll-body-under-scrutiny","title":"Explainer-Why India's Tens of Millions of Voter Deletions Have Led to Widespread Protests","description":"US News is a recognized leader in college, grad school, hospital, mutual fund, and car rankings. Track elected officials, research health conditions, and find news you can use in politics, business, health, and education.","language":"en","text":"# Explainer-Why India's Tens of Millions of Voter Deletions Have Led to Widespread Protests\n\nBy Reuters\n\n|\n\nSept. 28, 2026, at 8:16 a.m.\n\nExplainer-Why India's Tens of Millions of Voter Deletions Have Led to Widespread Protests\n\n*More*\n\nReuters\n\nFILE PHOTO: Members of security forces stand guard at the entrance of Election Commission of India headquarters on the day of a protest by the supporters of India’s main opposition Congress party demanding the resignation of Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar, in New Delhi, India, September 25, 2026. REUTERS/Bhawika Chhabra/File Photo\n\nBy Arpan Chaturvedi and Krishna N. Das\n\nNEW DELHI, Oct 9 (Reuters) - India's verification exercise of its nearly one billion voters has sparked ⁠protests ⁠across the country, with opposition parties alleging the removal of 130 million ⁠names from electoral rolls has unfairly benefited Prime Minister Narendra Modi's party.\n\nOpposition parties and the youth-led Cockroach Janta Party are demanding the ouster of Election Commission chief Gyanesh Kumar and have vowed a huge protest in the capital Delhi on Saturday.\n\nElection Commission data cited by Modi's Bharatiya Janata Party shows about 130 million names, more than the population of Japan, have been deleted from electoral rolls during the verification exercise, including 63 million people who had relocated and 30 million who could not be traced.\n\nThe BJP and the Commission deny allegations of manipulation, although opposition parties say Muslim voters and other minorities, who are unlikely to support the Hindu nationalist ruling party, are disproportionately impacted by the deletions.\n\nThey say the deletions have ⁠affected the outcome of some ⁠recent state elections that the BJP won, and have cast a shadow on the electoral process in the world's biggest democracy. The BJP says Modi's popularity and India's economic growth are some reasons for their electoral dominance.\n\nHere are some details about the voter verification drive, which has become one of the biggest political controversies of Modi's 12-year tenure ahead of crucial state elections starting early next year.\n\nWHAT'S HAPPENING?\n\nSupporters of the Cockroach Janta Party youth movement, farmers' groups and opposition parties are set to protest in Delhi on Saturday, demanding the resignation of Kumar. Police say they have not granted permission, and CJP has reported preemptive detentions of some protest leaders.\n\nSporadic protests have been going on across India since a report by the Indian Express newspaper in late September said Kumar's two deputies raised several concerns internally about voter list revisions, registration procedures and digital systems.\n\nThe Commission says ⁠differing ⁠views are normal in internal deliberations, and has ⁠denied favouring any party.\n\nModi's BJP has accused groups opposed to it of trying to undermine the country's democracy by wrongly questioning legitimate electoral processes.\n\nWHY DOES IT MATTER?\n\nAt least seven Indian states will hold elections next year, including BJP-ruled Uttar Pradesh, the most populous and considered a bellwether state in national politics. Verification ⁠in Uttar Pradesh began in October 2025 and resulted in 13% of names deleted, according to local media reports.\n\nVoters with their names missing need to prove their credentials before being enrolled, and many have complained about the process.\n\nVERIFYING A BILLION VOTERS: THE CONTROVERSY\n\nIndia started verification of its voter rolls last year ahead of elections in the eastern state of Bihar, ruled by a BJP coalition. The review has now covered the majority of Indian states.\n\nWhile voter roll revisions are routine, this round became controversial after large numbers of voters in states such as Bihar and West Bengal found their names missing from electoral rolls and were unable to cast ballots. The BJP won power in West Bengal for the first time in May.\n\nCiting Election Commission data, ⁠the BJP says that of the 130 million names deleted from voter lists, 28 million are dead, 63 million have relocated, 10 million are duplicates and 30 million ⁠could not be traced by officials.\n\nOpposition parties including Congress have accused Kumar of bias and alleged that the process benefits the BJP, charges the Election Commission and the party deny.\n\nThe Indian Express story said the two deputies to the election commissioner had expressed concerns to Kumar at least 14 times in 10 months but those concerns were not taken into consideration. The Election Commission said all its decisions were lawful.\n\nThe report prompted opposition leaders and members of the youth-led Cockroach Janta Party, which in July forced the education minister to quit after weeks of protest, to launch nationwide demonstrations.\n\nWHAT HAPPENS TO VOTERS REMOVED FROM THE ROLLS?\n\nThe Election Commission says names are only removed when a voter has died, is mentioned more than once on the rolls, has moved to another region or is untraceable. Officers are required to record detailed reasons why a voter is deleted.\n\nPeople whose names do not appear in draft rolls can request inclusion before final voter lists are published and also raise appeals if such requests are denied.\n\nVoters excluded from final rolls can also challenge the decision.\n\nTo participate in future elections, excluded voters must succeed in their appeals or apply for fresh registration.\n\nOpposition parties argue that arbitrary deletions and lengthy appeals prevented millions in Bihar and ⁠West Bengal from voting and could be used as a weapon in future elections.\n\nELECTION COMMISSION'S DEFENCE\n\nThe Election Commission has defended itself from allegations of political bias, including at a rare press conference by Kumar last year, who asked the opposition to submit their allegations under oath.\n\nKumar has had a difficult relationship with opposition parties since taking over as the election body chief in February 2025. Opposition lawmakers put forward a motion to have him removed earlier this year but it was rejected by parliament.\n\nA 2023 law passed by Modi's government grants the election commission chief immunity from prosecution for acts done in an official capacity.\n\nAmid the recent backlash, the Election Commission announced revisions to the verification process including home visits by officials to collect documents and an extension of the deadline for final verification in some states.\n\n(Reporting by Arpan Chaturvedi and Krishna N. Das; Editing by Alexandra Hudson and Raju Gopalakrishnan)\n\n**Copyright 2026 Thomson Reuters**.\n\nTags: India\n\nRead More","author":"Reuters","published_date":"2026-09-28T08:16-0400","latency_ms":25175.214801998663,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}